  "query_preview": "../Sunday through Friday/...\nA fairly active pattern will continue for Southeast Alaska \nthrough the long term period. A significant low pressure system \nmoving northward toward the area late Saturday and into Sunday. \nThis system currently appears to be the most worthy particularly \nfor the southern panhandle, with increasing forecast confidence in\nstrong winds (40-50mph) for areas south of Port Alexander. The \nuncertainty on the exact strength of this system is due to how \nquickly and where the low that moves through on Friday dies out. \nIf the Friday low weakens and dies out over by PWS before the late\nSaturday-Sunday system moves in then the southern panhandle could\nsee sustained SE winds closer to the 45-55mph. In addition to \nwind this low will bring a round of rain and high elevations snow \n(including the Haines and Klondike Highways). This low will \ncontinue to move north and then west toward Prince William Sound \nlate Sunday and into Monday and weaken. Multiple shortwaves after \nthis system will move through the area each brining their own \nbouts of preciptiation. What is more noteworthy during this period\nis the gradual lowering of snow levels through the rest of the \nweek. Current guidance even has snow levels dropping down to \naround 1000 feet with for areas along the Coastal Range and the \nnorthern Panhandle late next week. As a result if precipitation \nwere to be also moving through due to a shortwave disturbance \nthere would be the potential for it to fall as snow or rain/snow \nmix at sea level. All that said there is still great uncertainty \nwhen it comes to that time frame and multiple things would have to\nalign, but it is certainly something to watch.",
